<CD xmlns="http://www.openmath.org/OpenMathCD">
  <CDName>pmquant</CDName>
  <CDBase>http://www.plurimath.org/cd</CDBase>
  <CDDate>2026-11-19</CDDate>
  <CDStatus>experimental</CDStatus>
  <CDUses>
    <CDName>sts</CDName>
  </CDUses>
  <CDVersion>1</CDVersion>
  <CDRevision>1</CDRevision>
  <Description>
    The PM quantity and uncertainty constructors: the measurement-uncertainty
    categories of PM 1002 (the standard, expanded and coverage-interval
    categories of JCGM 100/101) as OpenMath symbols. The category is part of
    the constructed value's identity; converting between categories is a
    computation (PM 1004), never a rendering act. This CD is the Plurimath PM
    register's own content dictionary; it is experimental and loads beside
    the official collection. Revision 1 adds the constraint symbols of PM
    1012: the limits, the decision rules and the constraint evaluation.
  </Description>
  <CDDefinition>
    <Name>ustd</Name>
    <Role>application</Role>
    <Description>
      The standard uncertainty category (JCGM 100 7.2.1, PM 1002 clause 6):
      ustd(v, m) reports the result v with its standard uncertainty m. It
      carries value and magnitude; it carries no coverage factor, no
      coverage probability and no endpoints.
    </Description>
    <CMP>
      The constructed quantity keeps the category, the reported value and
      the magnitude exactly as given: exact operands stay exact. A magnitude
      must be non-negative and finite; a negative magnitude refuses.
    </CMP>
    <Example>ustd(10.3, 0.2)</Example>
  </CDDefinition>
  <CDDefinition>
    <Name>uexp</Name>
    <Role>application</Role>
    <Description>
      The expanded uncertainty category (JCGM 100 6.2.1 and 7.2.3, PM 1002
      clause 6): uexp(v, m, k) reports the result v with expanded-uncertainty
      magnitude m and coverage factor k, optionally with the coverage
      probability p that was used to choose k (uexp(v, m, k, p)). The
      magnitude here is the expanded uncertainty itself, not a standard
      uncertainty.
    </Description>
    <CMP>
      The coverage factor is a positive unsigned number: 0, 0.5 and negative
      factors do not describe coverage factors and refuse. The coverage
      probability, when present, lies strictly between 0 and 1; both bounds
      are exact comparisons, not float comparisons.
    </CMP>
    <Example>uexp(10.3, 0.5, 2, 0.95)</Example>
  </CDDefinition>
  <CDDefinition>
    <Name>ucov</Name>
    <Role>application</Role>
    <Description>
      The coverage interval category (JCGM 101 5.3 and 5.5, PM 1002 clause
      6): ucov(v, l, u) reports the result v with the interval [l, u];
      ucov(v, l, u, p) carries the coverage probability p; ucov(v, l, u, p,
      kind) carries the interval kind, the string "shortest" or "symmetric"
      (the probabilistically-symmetric kind; AsciiMath spells it symmetric
      because the dash would read as minus). The kind defaults to symmetric.
    </Description>
    <CMP>
      The lower endpoint must not exceed the upper one. The coverage
      probability, when present, lies strictly between 0 and 1. The two
      kinds differ semantically - the shortest interval of a distribution
      need not be its probabilistically symmetric one - and the constructed
      value carries the one kind it was given.
    </CMP>
    <Example>ucov(10.3, 9.9, 10.7, 0.95, shortest)</Example>
  </CDDefinition>
  <CDDefinition>
    <Name>value</Name>
    <Role>application</Role>
    <Description>
      The reported value of a quantity: value(q) extracts the reported value
      v of the uncertain quantity q without touching its uncertainty.
    </Description>
    <CMP>
      Applying value to a plain numeric quantity is the identity; applying
      it to an uncertain quantity gives the reported value with the exact
      class preserved.
    </CMP>
    <Example>value(ustd(10.3, 0.2))</Example>
  </CDDefinition>
  <CDDefinition>
    <Name>upper_limit</Name>
    <Role>application</Role>
    <Description>
      An upper limit (PM 1012 clause 5): upper_limit(L) states the boundary
      L with the comparison direction "at or below". A result satisfies it
      when the result does not exceed L.
    </Description>
    <CMP>
      The boundary is a number; exact boundaries stay exact.
    </CMP>
    <Example>upper_limit(100)</Example>
  </CDDefinition>
  <CDDefinition>
    <Name>lower_limit</Name>
    <Role>application</Role>
    <Description>
      A lower limit (PM 1012 clause 5): lower_limit(L) states the boundary
      L with the comparison direction "at or above". A result satisfies it
      when the result is not below L.
    </Description>
    <CMP>
      The boundary is a number; exact boundaries stay exact.
    </CMP>
    <Example>lower_limit(10)</Example>
  </CDDefinition>
  <CDDefinition>
    <Name>simple_acceptance</Name>
    <Role>application</Role>
    <Description>
      The simple acceptance decision rule (PM 1012 clause 6):
      simple_acceptance() lets the boundary comparison decide. There is no
      guard band and no conditional verdict.
    </Description>
    <CMP>
      The verdict is satisfied when the reported value of the result is
      within the limit, and not-satisfied otherwise. A result exactly at
      the boundary is within the limit.
    </CMP>
    <Example>simple_acceptance()</Example>
  </CDDefinition>
  <CDDefinition>
    <Name>guarded_acceptance</Name>
    <Role>application</Role>
    <Description>
      The guarded acceptance decision rule (PM 1012 clause 6, the ILAC-G8
      family): guarded_acceptance(k) sets the guard band to k times the
      standard uncertainty of the result. For an upper limit L the
      conditional zone is [L - k u, L]; for a lower limit it is
      [L, L + k u]. Both ends of the zone belong to it.
    </Description>
    <CMP>
      The coverage factor k is a positive number. The rule applies to
      results of the standard uncertainty category (ustd); other results
      refuse. A result outside the limit is not-satisfied, a result inside
      the zone is conditional, and any other result is satisfied.
    </CMP>
    <Example>guarded_acceptance(2)</Example>
  </CDDefinition>
  <CDDefinition>
    <Name>constraint</Name>
    <Role>application</Role>
    <Description>
      The constraint evaluation (PM 1012 clause 5): constraint(limit,
      result, rule) evaluates one result against one limit under one
      decision rule and gives a constraint result: the verdict
      (satisfied, not-satisfied or conditional), the signed residual, the
      rule and the diagnostics. constraint(limit, result, rule, guard)
      also takes the applicability guard (PM 1012 clause 8), a boolean
      evaluated through the same path as any other expression.
    </Description>
    <CMP>
      The signed residual is result - L in the limit's own number:
      negative for a result under an upper limit, positive for a result
      over a lower one. Under guarded acceptance the residual carries its
      expanded uncertainty (k u at factor k), and the result states the
      band and the zone. A guard that is false records the constraint as
      inapplicable; it is not an error. A guard that is not a boolean
      refuses.
    </CMP>
    <Example>constraint(upper_limit(100), ustd(99.7, 0.5), guarded_acceptance(2))</Example>
  </CDDefinition>
</CD>
